  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{x}-x-1}=$",
  "worked_solution": "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{x}-x-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[{x}^{2}]^{\u2032}}{[{e}^{x}-x-1]^{\u2032}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x}{{e}^{x}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x-1+{e}^{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x}{{e}^{x}-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[2x]^{\u2032}}{[{e}^{x}-1]^{\u2032}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
